※ pl4g-test: compile=ok run=native,qemu exit=42 diag=none

※ An array written down where one of no stated length is wanted makes the array
※ with the length in it and then lets the length go, which is the same thing
※ that happens to a name of a fixed type handed to such a parameter.  What the
※ elements are comes from the type that is wanted, so a literal with no suffix
※ stands as an element here as it does anywhere else.
※
※ The elements are lowered once.  Where the type says what they are they are
※ lowered by the writing into the run; where nothing says, they say it
※ themselves and what they came to is carried to the writing rather than worked
※ out again -- which is what keeps a call written as an element from being made
※ twice.

fn first(xs: u6⟦⟧) → u6:
    xs⟦0⟧

fn forty() → u6:
    40u6

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    ※ given to a name whose type does not say the length
    let named: u6⟦⟧ = ⟦40u6, 2u6⟧ in ⎕static
    ※ handed to a parameter whose type does not say it
    let handed: u6 = first(⟦1u6, 2u6⟧)
    ※ an element that is a call, which is made once
    let called: u6 = first(⟦forty(), 2u6⟧)
    named⟦1⟧ + handed - named⟦1⟧ + called + 1u6
